---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout
  title="Touch, pen, keyboard, and trackpad — Varve Docs"
  description="Using Varve on a Chromebook or tablet: touch gestures, USI pen behavior and what is unverified, virtual keyboard, ChromeOS-reserved shortcuts, French/English layouts, trackpad, and accessibility."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Touch, pen, keyboard, trackpad
      </nav>

      <h1 class="site-page-title">Touch, pen, keyboard, and trackpad</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Varve has one input model across laptop and tablet: pointer events are normalized
        once, screen-space thresholds stay in CSS pixels, and every gesture has a
        non-gesture alternative. This page covers what is implemented, what is
        regression-tested with emulated devices, and what still requires a physical
        Chromebook.
      </p>

      <aside class="docs-note">
        <strong>Hardware status:</strong> the behaviors below are covered by automated
        emulated-viewport and synthetic touch/pen tests. The Lenovo USI Pen 2's pressure
        range, tilt, eraser end, barrel button, hover, and palm behavior have{' '}
        <strong>not</strong> been measured on real hardware. Treat pen details as
        unverified until a device run is recorded.
      </aside>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Laptop and tablet workflows</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>With the detachable keyboard:</strong> a Chromebook behaves like a small laptop. Trackpad and keyboard shortcuts work as documented; the touchscreen stays active at the same time.</li>
          <li><strong>Tablet mode:</strong> attach/detach changes the layout without remounting the editor. Portrait switches supplementary panels to bottom sheets; landscape keeps side drawers. Rotation and detach preserve the document, selection, and undo history.</li>
          <li><strong>Back gesture:</strong> a left-edge swipe in ChromeOS tablet mode is browser Back. Varve intercepts it to dismiss the top dialog or menu first, exactly as <kbd>Esc</kbd> does, instead of leaving the document.</li>
          <li><strong>Split screen:</strong> the layout reflows to the narrow pane instead of forcing a desktop minimum width.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Touch gestures</h2>
        <table class="input-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Action</th><th>Gesture</th><th>Alternative</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Use the active tool</td><td>One finger drag by default; switch to one-finger viewport navigation in Settings &gt; Drawing input</td><td>Keyboard shortcuts, command palette, or the Hand tool</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pan the canvas</td><td>Two-finger drag</td><td>Hand tool, trackpad two-finger scroll, scrollbars/space-drag where available</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Zoom</td><td>Two-finger pinch</td><td>Status-bar zoom field, View menu, keyboard zoom, Zoom tool</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Recover from a second contact</td><td>The first provisional tool contact is cancelled for that pointer; both contacts become navigation-owned</td><td>Lift both fingers and start a fresh drawing contact</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Move or resize</td><td>Drag a selection or handle</td><td>Arrow-key nudge, Inspector position/size fields</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Context actions</td><td>Long-press (about 0.5 s) for the deep-selection menu</td><td>Right-click / two-finger tap with a trackpad or mouse</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Multi-select</td><td>Enable the touch multi-select toggle, then marquee</td><td>Shift/⌘-click with a pointer, Select menu</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          The drag threshold is 3 CSS pixels and does not scale with zoom, so a firm tap
          never nudges a shape and a deliberate drag never needs exaggerated travel. Page
          pinch-zoom is disabled over the canvas only; browser zoom stays available
          everywhere else.
        </p>
        <p>
          The standard wheel policy pans with a plain wheel and zooms with Ctrl/Cmd+wheel.
          Settings &gt; Drawing input provides a reversible pan/zoom override, sensitivity, and
          optional detented-mouse continuation; trackpad and ambiguous momentum remain direct-only.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Drawing input settings</h2>
        <p>
          Open <strong>Settings &gt; Drawing input</strong> to choose whether one-finger
          touch follows the active tool or navigates the viewport. The setting is local,
          reversible, and also applies when a WebView reports a custom or empty pointer type;
          Varve does not strand the canvas in a pen-only mode. Two-finger navigation always
          takes ownership without calling global Undo, creating history entries, or allowing
          the remaining finger to resume a stroke unexpectedly.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same panel enables pressure interpretation and adjusts its curve. If a runtime
          or device does not provide useful pressure data, brushes remain usable with constant
          width/opacity. A default <code>0.5</code> sample is not treated as proof that a pen
          has a pressure sensor. Predicted samples can improve a transient preview, but are
          never saved, exported, or added to Undo history.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Pen</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Pressure:</strong> stylus input is normalized with Pointer Events; a stylus that reports useful pressure passes it through to brushes. When pressure is unavailable or disabled, brushes use a stable constant response rather than becoming unusable.</li>
          <li><strong>Tilt and twist:</strong> the event model carries <code>tiltX</code>/<code>tiltY</code>, <code>twist</code>, and altitude/azimuth where the hardware reports them. Whether the USI Pen 2 reports any of these in Chrome is unmeasured.</li>
          <li><strong>Eraser end and barrel button:</strong> event state is preserved where the runtime exposes it, but behavior on the Lenovo USI Pen 2 is not verified on real hardware. If your pen's eraser or button does not behave as expected, report it with the device model and route.</li>
          <li><strong>Palm rejection and hover:</strong> left to ChromeOS and the digitizer; Varve does not synthesize or override them, and they are unverified.</li>
          <li><strong>Drawing:</strong> pressure sensitivity applies to brush tools; vector tools always place document-space geometry regardless of pressure.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Virtual keyboard</h2>
        <p>
          On Chromium-based browsers, Varve reads the virtual-keyboard geometry (the
          VirtualKeyboard API and its CSS environment insets where available, with{' '}
          <code>VisualViewport</code> as the fallback). Dialogs, toasts, and the panel
          drawer buttons reserve the reported height so the caret and commit/cancel
          controls stay visible and the editor returns to its previous size when the
          keyboard closes.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a specific field is still covered, dismiss and refocus it so the operating
          system scrolls it back into view. Text editing never depends on keyboard
          detection: a hardware keyboard or an external one always works.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Keyboard and ChromeOS conflicts</h2>
        <p>
          ChromeOS reserves some combinations before a web page sees them. The important
          ones for Varve:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd>…<kbd>8</kbd> switches browser tabs, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd>/<kbd>W</kbd>/<kbd>N</kbd> manage tabs and windows.</li>
          <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>+</kbd>/<kbd>-</kbd>/<kbd>0</kbd> changes the screen resolution, and <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>=</kbd>/<kbd>-</kbd> maximizes/minimizes windows.</li>
          <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>B</kbd> toggles the bookmarks bar. Three-finger swipes are OS tab/overview gestures, and two-finger tap or <kbd>Alt</kbd>+click is right-click.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          No essential Varve action is reachable only through an OS-reserved combination:
          workspace switching uses <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd>…<kbd>7</kbd>,
          and zoom is available from the status bar, menus, and keyboard. The full list is
          in <a href={sitePath('/docs/keyboard-shortcuts')}>Keyboard Shortcuts</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          On French and English Chromebook keyboards the zoom keys are matched by physical
          key position, so they behave the same on both layouts. Dead keys, AltGr
          combinations, and accent composition with the detachable keyboard have not been
          verified on a device; if a composition fails, report it with the layout name.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Trackpad</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Two-finger scroll pans the canvas; a trackpad pinch zooms it. Page zoom is not triggered over the canvas.</li>
          <li>Momentum scrolling does not double-apply; the gesture classifier distinguishes detented and high-resolution wheels.</li>
          <li>Tap-to-click, two-finger tap (right-click), and the clickpad's physical click all work; three- and four-finger swipes stay with ChromeOS.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Runtime routes</h2>
        <table class="input-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Route</th><th>What this guide covers</th><th>What still needs device evidence</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Chrome browser</td><td>Pointer ownership, touch controls, local persistence, and browser zoom/shortcut boundaries</td><td>Physical USI pressure, palm rejection, hover, and exact ChromeOS dispatch</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Installed web app/PWA</td><td>The same web input contract in its installed window, subject to the active Chromium version</td><td>Install-specific update/reload and device behavior</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Linux desktop in ChromeOS</td><td>Separate Tauri/WebKitGTK route with its own native file permissions and renderer</td><td>ARM64 package availability, WebKitGTK pointer capabilities, multitouch, pressure, and Crostini scaling</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          A successful browser test or Linux launch does not certify the other route. The Linux
          application uses the system WebKitGTK webview rather than Chrome, so pressure and
          multitouch must be checked separately. Varve does not advertise an Android app as a
          substitute for either route.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Accessibility</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Target size:</strong> the project targets 44 CSS px for primary controls on coarse pointers and holds the WCAG 2.2 AA minimum of 24×24 CSS px (or the spacing exception) for dense chrome. The requirement is independent of zoom.</li>
          <li><strong>No gesture-only actions:</strong> every drag and multipoint gesture has a single-pointer or keyboard equivalent, per WCAG 2.5.1 and 2.5.7.</li>
          <li><strong>Keyboard:</strong> panels and dialogs trap focus, return it on close, and close on <kbd>Esc</kbd>. The skip link and landmarks are present.</li>
          <li><strong>Screen readers:</strong> ChromeVox works in native Chrome. Google documents ChromeVox as supported only for the Terminal inside the Linux environment, so the Linux-app route is not a screen-reader path.</li>
          <li><strong>Reduced motion, contrast, and forced colors:</strong> authored animations are suppressed or simplified under reduced-motion, and the interface honors high-contrast and forced-color modes. See the <a href={sitePath('/accessibility')}>accessibility statement</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Related information</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/chromebook')}>Varve on Chromebook</a> ·
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/keyboard-shortcuts')}>Keyboard Shortcuts</a> ·
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/browser-demo')}>Browser Demo &amp; Offline</a> ·
          <a href={sitePath('/support/troubleshooting')}>Troubleshooting</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }

  .breadcrumb a,
  .docs-section a,
  .docs-cta a,
  .docs-note a {
    color: var(--text-link);
  }

  .docs-page h1 { font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }

  .docs-note {
    margin: -1rem 0 3rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary);
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .docs-note strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  .docs-section code {
    font-size: 0.875em;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-pill);
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .docs-section kbd {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.1em 0.4em;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-bottom-width: 2px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-control-compact);
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-size: 0.8125em;
    font-family: inherit;
  }

  .input-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--type-interface-control-size);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .input-table th,
  .input-table td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 0.65rem 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .input-table th {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .input-table td:first-child {
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .input-table {
      display: block;
      overflow-x: auto;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
  }
</style>
